findings.body:
`IBookingRepository.updateMany` is declared as returning `Promise<{ count: number }>`. `BookingRepository.updateMany` is declared with no return and resolves to `void`, so `BookingRepository implements IBookingRepository` fails to typecheck.

The Prisma `updateMany` result is also discarded, which hides the count. The method should `return` the result so the interface contract holds.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/repositories/BookingRepository.ts" lines="1502-1507"}

`handler` takes an optional `dependencies` argument. When it is missing, the function builds all five repositories from the global `prisma` import. `BookingCancelService` always passes its deps, so production uses the injected path. But the default export `handler` is what every existing test imports, so the tests run through the fallback and never exercise the injected path.

The result is two code paths that can drift apart without a failing test. If the DI wiring is wrong, the fallback quietly masks it. The PR description says the fallback exists for "transition", but nothing in the code marks it as temporary. The original `TODO` about removing the `handler` export was also deleted.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/lib/handleCancelBooking.ts" lines="139-153"}

`PrismaBookingAttendeeRepository.ts` is a near-copy of the existing `packages/features/bookings/repositories/AttendeeRepository.ts`, which is in the same folder. The new file also breaks repo style: it uses single quotes and omits semicolons, while the rest of the file set uses double quotes and semicolons. `Dependencies` in `handleCancelBooking.ts` then types `attendeeRepository` as the concrete class instead of the `IBookingAttendeeRepository` interface added in this PR, unlike the other four dependencies.

If the existing `AttendeeRepository` already covers `deleteManyByBookingId`, it should be extended rather than duplicated. If not, the new class should follow the same conventions as the other repositories in the folder.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/repositories/PrismaBookingAttendeeRepository.ts" lines="1-15"}

`BookingReferenceRepository` and `ProfileRepository` now take `{ prismaClient }` in their constructors. Their existing static methods still import and use the global `prisma`. A single class now reads from two sources of database access depending on how it is called.

Both classes also store the client twice: `private prismaClient` next to a `deps` parameter property in the reference repo. Callers can't tell which path is safe to use with a transaction client. This is hard to reason about when the cancel flow runs inside a transaction.

